WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.790
This video was made possible by Morning Brew.

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:07.259
Subscribe to their free daily newsletter at
the link in the description to make a daily

00:00:07.259 --> 00:00:10.940
habit of learning what’s going on in the
world.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:16.180
This is a map of the world, these are its
countries, this is their GDP per capita, and

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:17.970
that’s the equator.

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:21.320
With a mere glance, a pattern emerges.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:27.570
This half of the world—the southern hemisphere—is
poorer than this half—the northern hemisphere.

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:33.080
Of course, GDP is an imperfect indicator,
but even when you swap to another—like the

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:37.670
United Nations’ Human Development Index—the
pattern holds.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:42.399
The southern hemisphere is, on average, less
developed than the north.

00:00:42.399 --> 00:00:48.930
In fact, of the fifty-eight countries the
World Bank classifies as “high income,”

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:53.750
a mere five—Chile, Uruguay, the Seychelles,
Australia, and New Zealand—are located south

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:54.929
of the equator.

00:00:54.929 --> 00:00:59.960
If you look at the entire global distribution
of wealth and average it to find the center—the

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:05.220
world’s economic center of gravity—that
point is located here—northeast of Moscow,

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.820
at roughly the same latitude as Stockholm.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:14.550
That center of wealth is pulled to this point
by the world’s three main economic centers:

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:18.590
southeast Asia, western Europe, and North
America.

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:23.950
The southern hemisphere, representing just
11% of the world’s population, has little

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:29.250
economic sway, meager political influence,
and has never, in modern history, enjoyed

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:33.380
a similar level of development to its northern
counterpart.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:36.330
So… why?

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:38.850
The quick answer is we don’t know.

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:44.700
As any phenomenon grows in size, an accurate
explanation grows increasingly difficult.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:49.890
This phenomenon spans the entirety of the
world’s geography, the entirety of modern

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:53.360
history, and the entirety of the global economy.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.720
Therefore, any explanation is mere theory.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:03.580
Theories are not fact, but rather represent
a strong consensus in academic thinking.

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:09.489
That being said, through time, theories can
be proved flawed, limited, or flat-out false.

00:02:09.489 --> 00:02:15.150
In fact, in the past hundred years, the way
in which we think about how geography correlates

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:17.930
to wealth has changed fundamentally.

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:25.310
Take, for example, this—the opening lines
of the 1915 book Civilization and Climate,

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:27.069
by Ellsworth Huntington.

00:02:27.069 --> 00:02:29.980
“The races of the earth are like trees.

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:34.209
Each according to its kind brings forth the
fruit known as civilization.

00:02:34.209 --> 00:02:37.920
As russet apples and pippins may grow from
the same trunk, and as peaches may even be

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:43.310
grafted on a plum tree, so the culture of
applied races may be transferred from one

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:44.310
to another.

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:49.810
Yet no one expects pears on cherry branches,
and it is useless to look for Slavic civilization

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:51.129
among the Chinese.”

00:02:51.129 --> 00:02:56.209
He goes on to liken the actions of humans
to a grower cultivating their vineyard—this

00:02:56.209 --> 00:03:01.829
can enhance a crop, but what matters more
is the geography of where the grapes grows.

00:03:01.829 --> 00:03:06.749
No matter how well cultivated, you’d never
expect good grapes from Greenland.

00:03:06.749 --> 00:03:11.370
Huntington posits that the two key factors
influencing the success of an vineyard are

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:17.310
its environment—which it inherited—and
its health—which is dependent on human action.

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:24.180
Huntington then asks, “Does the fruit known
as civilization depend upon these same conditions?

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:26.989
It seems to me that it does.

00:03:26.989 --> 00:03:31.120
Few would question that a race with a superb
mental and physical inheritance and endowed

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:36.010
with perfect health is capable of adding indefinitely
to the cultural inheritance received from

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:39.709
its ancestors, and thus may attain the highest
civilization.

00:03:39.709 --> 00:03:44.790
But if that same cultural inheritance were
given to a sickly race with a weak inheritance

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:49.159
of both mind and body, there would almost
surely be degeneration.”

00:03:49.159 --> 00:03:55.590
In short, Huntington argues that if one were
to give good land to, in his words, a “sickly

00:03:55.590 --> 00:04:00.129
race,” they would be incapable of properly
developing it.

00:04:00.129 --> 00:04:04.370
Rhetoric like this is what was used to justify
colonialism.

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:08.529
Further core arguments of the book include
theories positing that mental abilities correlate

00:04:08.529 --> 00:04:13.450
to average temperatures—the colder the climate,
the smarter its people are.

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:18.830
Therefore, in his logic, if you took people
with higher mental abilities, from the cooler

00:04:18.830 --> 00:04:23.520
areas, and brought them to manage the hotter
areas, they would be more successful, both

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:27.090
literally and figuratively, in cultivating
the land.

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:29.290
This is false.

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:32.280
This is also, unequivocally, racist.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:38.180
It’s no surprise that later in his career,
Huntington’s research devolved into flat-out

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:39.560
eugenics.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:44.390
We now know the belief that a hotter environment
makes people, quote, unquote, “uncivilized,”

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:47.200
as was once a prevailing view, is absurd.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:52.200
The temperature or physical environment of
a place does not change a person in any meaningful

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:53.200
way.

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:57.360
The reason this south-west corner of Egypt,
for example, is almost completely undeveloped

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:01.160
is not because the sun is degrading its people’s
mental abilities.

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:06.980
However, simultaneously, it would be absurd
to believe that geography does not play a

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:08.780
role in development.

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:12.850
We know, with some certainty, that the reason
this corner of Egypt is almost completely

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:19.030
undeveloped is because its in the Sahara Desert—with
inhumane heat, little to no water, and few

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:21.530
desirable natural resources.

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:26.390
The thin line between racism and legitimate
theory is what makes examining any correlation

00:05:26.390 --> 00:05:29.590
between geography and wealth so difficult.

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:34.390
However, a simple rule can delineate the two.

00:05:34.390 --> 00:05:39.640
Geography does not influence a person’s
ability to develop a place.

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:45.760
However, geography can influence a place’s
ability to be developed by people.

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:51.150
A place’s development potential is absolutely
influenced by geography.

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:57.470
Therefore, that poses a question: what does
the northern hemisphere have that the southern

00:05:57.470 --> 00:05:58.890
does not?

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:01.620
In short: latitude.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:06.150
Not only does the southern hemisphere have
about half as much land as the northern, but

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:09.520
that land does not stretch as far from the
equator.

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:14.860
For example, Cape Town, at the southern tip
of Africa, sits about 34 degrees south of

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:15.950
the equator.

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:20.990
In the northern hemisphere, cities at an equivalent
distance to the equator include Los Angeles

00:06:20.990 --> 00:06:25.770
or Baghdad—hardly what anyone would consider
“northern cities.”

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:29.100
So, what effect does this have?

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:32.750
This is a graph composed of the world’s
countries.

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:37.590
The higher a given country is, the wealthier
it is on a per capita basis, while the further

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:40.910
to the right it is, the further it sits from
the equator.

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:43.720
The pattern that emerges is decisive.

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:47.830
The further a country is from the equator,
the wealthier it is.

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:52.390
Therefore, a plausible reason for why the
southern hemisphere is poorer is simply that

00:06:52.390 --> 00:06:54.400
its landmass sits closer to the equator.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:07:01.020
But that’s not a full explanation, because
it then presents a new question: why are equatorial

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:03.310
countries poorer?

00:07:03.310 --> 00:07:08.280
Bounded by the perspective of our lifetimes,
its easy to forget how close we are in history

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:11.150
to an era dominated by agriculture.

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:16.780
Just 100 years ago, when the world was the
domain of many of our grandparents, 70% of

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:20.470
earth’s working population was employed
in agriculture.

00:07:20.470 --> 00:07:24.270
Today, that number is just 27%.

00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:30.220
As industry innovation and mechanization ramped
up, so too did per person productivity.

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:36.090
However, 27% is an average of a highly variable
dataset.

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:42.520
Today, 1.66% of the US’ labor force is employed
in agriculture.

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:46.240
86% of the Central African Republic’s is.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:52.410
This indicator represents one of the starkest
differences between rich and poor countries.

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:57.700
Places like the UK, France, Japan, and Australia
sit at low single-digits, while those like

00:07:57.700 --> 00:08:01.220
Mauritania, Madagascar, and Laos sit at high
double-digits.

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:08.600
Unsurprisingly, comparing this map to that
of GDP per capita, it tracks quite closely.

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:13.510
Countries with low GDP have high agricultural
employment percentages, and vice versa.

00:08:13.510 --> 00:08:19.130
So, does that mean poverty creates high agricultural
employment rates, or that high agricultural

00:08:19.130 --> 00:08:21.090
employment rates create poverty?

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:25.040
Or, rather: is this correlation or causation?

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:30.700
Well, according to the Food and Agriculture
Organization of the United Nations, 60% of

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:36.800
the calories humans consume are derived from
just three crops: maize,—or corn, as it’s

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:39.469
known in the US—rice, and wheat.

00:08:39.469 --> 00:08:44.790
Now, in agriculture, as any farmer will tell
you, climate is everything.

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:48.959
In the case of the wheat, the highest yield
temperature—as in, the temperature that

00:08:48.959 --> 00:08:54.389
leads to the largest harvest of crop per unit
of land—is about 74 degrees Fahrenheit,

00:08:54.389 --> 00:08:56.230
or 23 Celsius.

00:08:56.230 --> 00:09:01.440
Every degree Celsius above that, average wheat
yields decrease by 10%.

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:06.459
What this means is that more equatorial countries,
which tend to be hotter, have less productive

00:09:06.459 --> 00:09:07.680
wheat crops.

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:12.379
The Food and Agriculture Organization of the
United Nations created this map to show the

00:09:12.379 --> 00:09:17.209
average possible wheat yield around the world,
and unsurprisingly, it roughly correlates

00:09:17.209 --> 00:09:20.880
with the map of country’s agricultural labor
force proportion.

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:26.959
This makes sense: the less productive farmland
is, the more people you need to produce a

00:09:26.959 --> 00:09:28.699
person’s worth of food.

00:09:28.699 --> 00:09:33.800
The more people working to create food, the
less there are to innovate and grow an economy.

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:38.930
Therefore, this also correlates to the map
of country’s GDP per capita or human development

00:09:38.930 --> 00:09:40.170
index.

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:45.459
While every crop is different, in terms of
its ideal temperature, as a generalization,

00:09:45.459 --> 00:09:50.490
warm, but not hot, places are those best suited
for agriculture.

00:09:50.490 --> 00:09:55.819
Those warm but not hot places are overwhelmingly
located in the temperate zone of latitudes,

00:09:55.819 --> 00:09:59.180
which encompasses very little of the southern
hemisphere’s landmass.

00:09:59.180 --> 00:10:02.540
It’s a similar story with disease.

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:08.420
The burden of disease, while heavily skewed
by equatorial Africa, is, on average, higher

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:11.160
the closer to the equator a country is.

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:16.309
Malaria, for example, is one of the most deadly
infectious diseases in the world.

00:10:16.309 --> 00:10:20.779
Its prevalent predominantly in areas that
are hot year-round, have high humidity, and

00:10:20.779 --> 00:10:25.790
lots of rainfall which, overwhelmingly, represents
equatorial regions.

00:10:25.790 --> 00:10:30.790
Beyond Malaria, the lack of a cold season
in equatorial regions means that insect populations

00:10:30.790 --> 00:10:35.790
run rampant, and mosquitos, ticks, fleas,
and others are some of the most reliable disease

00:10:35.790 --> 00:10:37.480
vectors out there.

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:43.420
Now, of course, in addition to insect-born
diseases, more common in equatorial areas,

00:10:43.420 --> 00:10:47.420
there are those that clearly are more common
in poverty-stricken places.

00:10:47.420 --> 00:10:52.089
Diarrhea, for example, is a leading cause
of death worldwide, and its root cause is

00:10:52.089 --> 00:10:57.370
often contaminated drinking water, which is
more common in places with inadequate or nonexistent

00:10:57.370 --> 00:10:58.699
water infrastructure.

00:10:58.699 --> 00:11:04.730
Therefore, it’s clear that poverty can spur
disease, but less clear whether disease can

00:11:04.730 --> 00:11:06.370
spur poverty.

00:11:06.370 --> 00:11:10.360
This is a topic that has been debated in the
world of international development for decades,

00:11:10.360 --> 00:11:16.580
but a lose consensus has formed that suggests
the answer is yes: a higher burden of disease

00:11:16.580 --> 00:11:20.019
can negatively impact a national economy.

00:11:20.019 --> 00:11:23.459
Of course, this makes sense.

00:11:23.459 --> 00:11:27.449
Disease often means a person is unable to
work, which means they are no longer able

00:11:27.449 --> 00:11:28.980
to expand an economy.

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:34.829
So, its clear that around the equator, poverty
is increasing disease, and disease is increasing

00:11:34.829 --> 00:11:39.399
poverty, even if its unclear the extent to
which it’s one versus the other.

00:11:39.399 --> 00:11:44.899
So, correlative factors such as agricultural
yield, disease burden, and more certainly

00:11:44.899 --> 00:11:47.559
can cause a degree of poverty.

00:11:47.559 --> 00:11:52.230
However, it would be obtuse to think that
the incredible level of inequality in the

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:56.279
world—with the average Norwegian earning
an average annual Somalian income in just

00:11:56.279 --> 00:12:02.139
three days—can be entirely explained by
some heat, wheat, and bugs.

00:12:02.139 --> 00:12:07.149
More likely, these factors opened a gap that
has been widened through time.

00:12:07.149 --> 00:12:13.259
In the US’ National Hockey League, 31 teams
competed in the 2020 - 2021 season, and their

00:12:13.259 --> 00:12:15.360
performance varied dramatically.

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:21.160
At the upper end of that spectrum, the Washington
Capitals did quite well, having won some 36

00:12:21.160 --> 00:12:26.529
games, while the Columbus Blue Jackets did…
less well, having won just 18.

00:12:26.529 --> 00:12:31.360
The Washington Capitals was established, as
a team, in 1974, while the Blue Jackets was

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:32.829
formed in 1997.

00:12:32.829 --> 00:12:37.490
So here’s the question: is the reason the
Capitals are better than the Blue Jackets

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:40.009
because they’ve had more time to develop?

00:12:40.009 --> 00:12:44.370
That is, are the Blue Jackets fundamentally
as good as the Capitals, but just 23 years

00:12:44.370 --> 00:12:46.000
behind in their development?

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:51.129
Or rather, are the Blue Jackets performing
poorly because, in a sports league, by nature,

00:12:51.129 --> 00:12:54.839
some teams have to perform worse, and are
pushed into that position by certain teams

00:12:54.839 --> 00:13:00.050
acquiring the best players, hiring the best
coaches, and building the best facilities?

00:13:00.050 --> 00:13:04.180
Applying the same logic to countries, the
former theory would suggest that the reason

00:13:04.180 --> 00:13:09.209
why Russia is less developed than the UK is
because the UK experienced its industrial

00:13:09.209 --> 00:13:16.990
revolution from 1760 to 1820, while Russia
waited until 1850 to 1920 to have its in earnest.

00:13:16.990 --> 00:13:22.920
Therefore, with an 100 year head start, the
UK sits further along in the timeline of development.

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:28.180
For the longest time, this was the explanation
for inequality, and interventions involved

00:13:28.180 --> 00:13:33.010
attempting to speed up a country’s progression
in the supposed development timeline.

00:13:33.010 --> 00:13:37.339
Certain countries started their industrial
development first so now, in an era where

00:13:37.339 --> 00:13:43.240
industrialization is everything in an economy,
certain countries have a leg up.

00:13:43.240 --> 00:13:46.129
That explanation eventually fell out of favor.

00:13:46.129 --> 00:13:51.140
It was once thought that after Africa decolonized,
once any gains from industry would go to a

00:13:51.140 --> 00:13:55.720
country itself, rather than its colonizer,
that these places would start their process

00:13:55.720 --> 00:13:56.720
of development.

00:13:56.720 --> 00:13:58.689
But then they didn’t.

00:13:58.689 --> 00:14:04.300
Many African countries are still in as desperate
a state as fifty years ago, and simply aren’t

00:14:04.300 --> 00:14:05.980
developing at all.

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:11.350
Therefore, updated explanations have emerged—explanations
that would suggest that the reason certain

00:14:11.350 --> 00:14:16.440
countries are poor is not because of their
place in time, but rather their place in the

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:17.790
world economy.

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:21.029
Imagine there are just two countries.

00:14:21.029 --> 00:14:26.529
One has a GDP per capita of $10,000, the other
$20,000.

00:14:26.529 --> 00:14:31.199
The poorer country sells what we’ll call
primary goods—products that don’t require

00:14:31.199 --> 00:14:33.870
manufacturing, like agriculture and raw materials.

00:14:33.870 --> 00:14:37.420
The second country, however, sells manufactured
goods.

00:14:37.420 --> 00:14:41.660
They’ll take a tree and turn it into a stool,
for example.

00:14:41.660 --> 00:14:44.709
But now the richer country has invented ice
cream.

00:14:44.709 --> 00:14:48.029
Of course, people don’t need ice cream.

00:14:48.029 --> 00:14:51.209
People want ice cream, but they don’t need
it.

00:14:51.209 --> 00:14:55.819
Therefore, people only buy ice cream when
they have the money to do so—as in, when

00:14:55.819 --> 00:14:57.189
they’re wealthier.

00:14:57.189 --> 00:15:01.170
Because the wealthier country has now invented
a way to turn cheaper raw materials into a

00:15:01.170 --> 00:15:04.339
more expensive product, they’re now getting
richer.

00:15:04.339 --> 00:15:08.360
They’ll sell some of this within their own
country, some to the other country, and also

00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:12.350
buy some raw materials to make the ice cream
from the poorer country.

00:15:12.350 --> 00:15:16.509
Because the rich country is getting richer,
more people are able to buy that ice cream,

00:15:16.509 --> 00:15:18.350
so demand goes up.

00:15:18.350 --> 00:15:23.999
And as is economics 101, when demand for a
product goes up, but supply stays stable,

00:15:23.999 --> 00:15:26.110
so does the price.

00:15:26.110 --> 00:15:30.930
This cycle repeats itself—the rich country
might invent bikes, then lightbulbs, then

00:15:30.930 --> 00:15:35.610
eventually smartphones, and all the while,
its buying the materials to make these products

00:15:35.610 --> 00:15:39.589
from the poorer country—the one that sells
primary goods.

00:15:39.589 --> 00:15:44.069
Meanwhile, demand for primary goods has stayed
relatively stable.

00:15:44.069 --> 00:15:47.480
People buying manufactured goods aren’t
necessarily buying many more things, but just

00:15:47.480 --> 00:15:51.989
more expensive things, so the raw materials
industry is about stable, and with products

00:15:51.989 --> 00:15:57.560
like food, one person still buys about one
person’s worth of food, regardless of income.

00:15:57.560 --> 00:16:03.510
So, in summary, we know that demand for primary
goods, like food and raw materials, stays

00:16:03.510 --> 00:16:07.089
relatively stable regardless of a person’s
income.

00:16:07.089 --> 00:16:12.389
Demand for manufactured goods, however, increases
dramatically as one’s income does.

00:16:12.389 --> 00:16:16.929
Therefore, through time, the primary goods
country’s economy stays relatively stable,

00:16:16.929 --> 00:16:19.720
while the manufactured goods country’s economy
grows.

00:16:19.720 --> 00:16:25.899
All the while, the prices for manufactured
goods rise, as demand does, because the manufactured

00:16:25.899 --> 00:16:28.730
goods country’s people are becoming wealthier.

00:16:28.730 --> 00:16:33.559
Therefore, people in the poorer country are
increasingly less able to buy these manufactured

00:16:33.559 --> 00:16:37.100
goods, as their incomes have stayed relatively
stable.

00:16:37.100 --> 00:16:41.670
This is what’s theorized to be happening
in the real world, right now.

00:16:41.670 --> 00:16:46.110
We know that Papua New Guinea, for example,
cannot make iPhones.

00:16:46.110 --> 00:16:50.689
Their industrial sector just is not equipped
for the most advanced manufacturing.

00:16:50.689 --> 00:16:57.089
Therefore, their economy is more centered
around primary goods—its an extractive economy.

00:16:57.089 --> 00:17:01.769
Places like Japan, however, have developed
so much, and their wages have grown so much,

00:17:01.769 --> 00:17:06.380
that it rarely makes financial sense for them
to produce primary goods, rather than manufactured

00:17:06.380 --> 00:17:07.380
goods.

00:17:07.380 --> 00:17:12.980
Therefore, we can say that, in general, poorer
countries produce primary goods, richer countries

00:17:12.980 --> 00:17:15.240
produce manufactured goods.

00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:19.789
The total cost of the raw materials in an
iPhone, for example—as in, the materials

00:17:19.789 --> 00:17:24.760
before any manufacturing process—is just
a few dollars.

00:17:24.760 --> 00:17:30.830
Through advanced manufacturing, that is transformed
into something that sells for more than $1,000.

00:17:30.830 --> 00:17:36.029
Therefore, with this theory, it’s believed
that poorer, primary goods producing countries

00:17:36.029 --> 00:17:42.750
are serving a crucial role in the world economy,
but not one that creates significant profit.

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:48.049
As a result, they have no money to invest
in advancing their economy and are stuck producing

00:17:48.049 --> 00:17:49.700
primary goods.

00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:53.591
While this theory has lost some relevance
as outsourcing has become more common, its

00:17:53.591 --> 00:17:57.640
still the case that the companies profiting
from advanced manufacturing are overwhelmingly

00:17:57.640 --> 00:18:03.630
located in rich countries, even if the manufacturing
itself has moved somewhat to poorer countries.

00:18:03.630 --> 00:18:09.140
This is only a part of a complex and still
controversial theory, but at the crux of it

00:18:09.140 --> 00:18:13.990
is a simple belief: poorer countries are dependent.

00:18:13.990 --> 00:18:19.049
They create resources, and these resources
flow to richer countries, who reap the majority

00:18:19.049 --> 00:18:20.539
of their gains.

00:18:20.539 --> 00:18:25.340
While this was very much the case during the
era of colonialism, countries exited this

00:18:25.340 --> 00:18:28.669
era with largely the same economic system.

00:18:28.669 --> 00:18:33.049
Therefore, they were stuck making primary
goods, where they’re unable to accumulate

00:18:33.049 --> 00:18:36.250
significant capital and advance their economy.

00:18:36.250 --> 00:18:41.600
At the crux of it, according to this theory,
poor countries get poorer, and rich countries

00:18:41.600 --> 00:18:42.990
get richer.

00:18:42.990 --> 00:18:47.169
The countries closer to the equator, and those
that disproportionally make up the southern

00:18:47.169 --> 00:18:53.549
hemisphere, do have natural factors that legitimately
negatively impact their ability to develop

00:18:53.549 --> 00:18:54.840
economically.

00:18:54.840 --> 00:18:59.950
These factors very possibly were the origin
of the development gap between, for example,

00:18:59.950 --> 00:19:01.390
Europe and Africa.

00:19:01.390 --> 00:19:07.730
However, there is an increasing, albeit controversial,
consensus around the thought that our modern,

00:19:07.730 --> 00:19:12.970
global economic system might be working to
widen the gap between rich and poor.

00:19:12.970 --> 00:19:16.340
Nobody’s becoming a billionaire by running
a farm.

00:19:16.340 --> 00:19:19.440
They’re becoming a billionaire selling iPhones.

00:19:19.440 --> 00:19:24.809
Therefore, as some countries are stuck selling
food, minerals, and other raw materials to

00:19:24.809 --> 00:19:32.140
those that transform them into profit, their
cycle of poverty is bound to continue.

